The '''1924 Alpujarra earthquake''' ({{lang-bl|Terremoto de Alpujarra de 1924}}) occurred on 27 April 1924 at 11:36 a.m. local time, with a [[W:Moment magnitude scale|moment magnitude]] of 7.9 [[W:Moment magnitude scale|M<sub>w</sub>]]. The earthquake struck the densely populated [[Alpujarra]] valley of the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]], with its epicenter along the [[Yawar Sypa fault]]. It was one of the deadliest and most destructive seismic events in Baliscan history, resulting in approximately 58,928 fatalities and injuring over 226,000 people. The severe shaking triggered extensive firestorms throughout Ampurias, including a devastating [[W:Fire whirl|fire whirl]] that swept through the historic city center.

==Background==

The Alpujarra had experienced significant urban expansion during the late 19th and early 20th centuries as Balisca underwent rapid industrialization and [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration greatly increased]]. [[Ampurias]] and the wider metropolis was undergoing a boom in construction of [[W:Early skyscrapers|early skyscrapers]], while seismic risks were poorly understood at the time. The city featured a mix of heavy masonry structures from the colonial period and newer wooden frame buildings erected during the cooperative housing boom of the 1900s-1920s.

The Yawar Sypa fault, whose name derives from [[Kurupí language|Kurupí]] words meaning "blood" (''yawar'') and "river" (''sypa''), had been seismically quiescent for over three centuries prior to 1924. Early colonial records noted a "great shaking" in 1623, but subsequent settlers largely forgot the region's seismic history. By the 1920s, Alpujarra had become the commercial and financial heart of Castejón, with the [[Borsa Confederale]] (Confederal Stock Exchange) and numerous cooperative banking houses concentrated along the [[Tistaina Canal]].

==Earthquake==

===Seismology and intensity===

The earthquake ruptured approximately 120 kilometers of the Yawar Sypa fault at a shallow depth of 15 kilometers, producing intense ground shaking across the Castejón metropolitan area. The rupture propagated rapidly southward toward the city center, concentrating seismic energy in the densely populated Alpujarra and Ampurias districts. The maximum Modified Mercalli intensity of XI ("Extreme") was recorded in a zone extending from the epicenter through central Ampurias along the Tistaina Canal.

The oblique-slip reverse mechanism combined horizontal compression with vertical uplift, generating complex ground motions that proved particularly destructive to the unreinforced masonry structures prevalent in older neighborhoods. Peak ground acceleration reached 0.65 ''g'' at the epicenter, among the highest reliably recorded in an urban environment during the early 20th century. The duration of strong shaking, approximately 45-60 seconds, allowed progressive structural collapse of weakened buildings.

Five significant aftershocks of magnitude 5.3 or greater occurred in the subsequent 48 hours, hampering rescue efforts and causing additional damage to structurally compromised buildings. The largest aftershock, a magnitude 6.2 event on 28 April, triggered a major fire in the [[Llanito]] borough when damaged gas lines ignited, claimed an additional 2,400 lives.

===Geological effects===

The earthquake caused widespread ground rupturing along the Yawar Sypa fault trace, with vertical displacements of up to 3 meters observed in rural areas west and south of the Alpujarra. [[W:Soil liquefaction|Liquefaction of alluvial deposits]] along the Tistaina Canal undermined building foundations throughout the historic district, causing structures to tilt or sink into the liquefied soil. The canal itself experienced significant lateral spreading, with sections of the embankment collapsing into the waterway and temporarily blocking navigation.

In [[Salto Bay]], localized submarine landslides generated a minor tsunami that affected low-lying coastal areas of the [[Coruxón]] and [[Sástago]] boroughs. The tsunami, though modest at 2-3 meters height, compounded damage in areas already devastated by ground shaking and occurred approximately 20 minutes after the main shock, catching many survivors who had fled to the waterfront.

==Damage and casualties==
[[File:San Francisco Fire Sacramento Street 1906-04-18.jpg|thumb|Photograph from [[El País]], looking toward the fire below [[Almiserat]]]]
The earthquake and subsequent fires destroyed approximately 78,000 buildings in Castejón, rendering over 300,000 people homeless. The historic center of [[Ampurias]] suffered near-total destruction, with the colonial-era [[Casco Veijo]] district burning for three days. The Tistaina Canal waterfront, lined with heavy stone-masonry early towers and wooden warehouses, became a fire corridor when ruptured gas mains ignited spilled lamp oil and chemicals.

The [[Palacio Cajal]] building, long considered a symbol of Balisca's pre-earthquake prosperity, collapsed within minutes of the initial shock, killing 1,200 traders and clerks. The [[Palacio de los Argonautas]], the city's largest theater, crumbled during a morning matinee performance with approximately 900 fatalities. The [[Capilla de San Esteban]], dating to the 17th century, disintegrated completely, with only the baroque facade remaining precariously standing until aftershocks brought it down the following day.

Infrastructure damage extended throughout the metropolitan transit network. The [[Charxes Tramway]], an [[W:Aerial tramway|aerial tramway]] completed in 1912, collapsed when its cable anchorage failed, sending three passenger cars plummeting 40 meters to the street below. An elevated section of the [[Castejón Metro]]'s (then known as the Castejón Underground) [[Castejón Metro Iznájar Line|Iznájar Line]] also failed, plummeting into the street below and killing a further 62 people.

The fire whirl that developed in the intersection of the Tistaina Canal and [[Avenida de la República]] became the most infamous image of the disaster. Formed when superheated fire gases rose in a vortex pattern between collapsing masonry structures, the fire whirl reached estimated wind speeds of 160 kilometers per hour and temperatures exceeding {{Convert|1000|C|F}}. It moved erratically through the financial district for 45 minutes, cremating everything in its path and asphyxiating survivors who sought shelter in underground vaults.

=== Deaths and injuries ===
The death toll of 58,928 made the 1924 Alpujarra earthquake the deadliest disaster in Baliscan history, exceeding the combined military and civilian casualties of the [[Great Adonian War]] in the Castejón metropolitan area. Injury estimates of 226,781 overwhelmed the Confederal Health Service's emergency capacity, necessitating the establishment of field hospitals in parks and public buildings throughout the metropolitan region.

The densely populated working-class neighborhoods of [[Charxes]] and [[Coruxón]] suffered disproportionate casualties due to the prevalence of unsafe "cooperative tenements"—quickly constructed housing blocks that lacked structural reinforcement. In Coruxón alone, approximately 14,000 residents died as a result of entier city blocks being pancaked into the liquefied ground. The death toll would have been higher had the earthquake not struck on a Sunday morning, when many potential victims were at home rather than in collapsed factory or port facilities.

Mortuary services were overwhelmed, with bodies initially stored in the ruined [[Mercado de la Torreón]] until temporary mass graves could be excavated in the outskirts of [[Alquézar]]. The Confederal government declared a state of emergency lasting 18 months, suspending normal constitutional procedures and martialing all cooperative resources toward relief and reconstruction.

==Response and relief==
{{Main|Estado Nuevo}}
[[File:Row of shacks, 1906 earthquake in San Francisco.jpg|thumb|315x315px|"Relief cottage" camp at the edge of [[Charxes]] in 1925]]
The [[Confederal Congress]] immediately appropriated 450 million [[Baliscan sol|sol]] for emergency relief, establishing the [[Comisión de Reconstrucción de Castejón]] (Castejón Reconstruction Commission) with broad powers to coordinate cooperative resources. The [[Confederal Health Service]], still in its infancy following the [[Nationalization of Healthcare Act]] (1922), mobilized  available medical practitioners from across the country despite lacking facilities or supplies for mass casualties.

International aid arrived within days from [[Volisania]], [[Asikyira]], and the [[Burawa|Kingdom of Burawa]], though the scale of destruction strained Castejón's port capacity. Ships were diverted to [[Jatiel]], [[Yerba Buena]] and [[Alessandria]], with supplies transported overland by rail and cooperative trucking networks. The [[Baliscan Red Cross]] established its modern organizational structure during the relief operations, becoming a permanent fixture of emergency response.

Temporary housing for displaced populations was constructed using standardized "relief cottages" designed by the [[Cooperative Architects' Guild]]—small, one-room structures of wood and corrugated metal that could be erected in hours. The [[Confederal Army]], [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]] and dozens of cooperatives were involved in the relief efforts, and over 80,000 such cottages dotted Castejón's parks and plazas for three years while permanent reconstruction proceeded.

=== Reconstruction ===
The Reconstruction Commission imposed strict new building codes requiring reinforced masonry and limiting building heights in liquefaction-prone zones. The historic [[Casco Veijo]] was rebuilt with wider streets and mandatory firebreaks, though preservationists successfully lobbied for the restoration of the district's pre-earthquake street plan wherever structurally feasible. 

As the Metropolitan authorities attempted to rebuild the devastated capital city they initially drafted a comprehensive reconstruction plan, but soon concluded that they lacked the budget to carry it out. And so, in areas where neither the metropolitan government nor the country’s real-estate and transportation cooperatives could properly fund reconstruction efforts, whole neighborhoods instead rapidly rebuilt themselves. Working on a small scale, residents [[W:Building cooperative|rebuilt homes and shops using scraped-together funds while relying on little more than their collective grit and inventiveness]], and [[W:Black market|black markets]] full of micro-entrepreneurs sprung up around the city’s major train stations. These neighborhoods were not initially planned, per se—they ''emerged'', and their ramshackle, spontaneous spirit can still be felt today when walking Castejón's [[W:Alley|backstreets]].

This approach was adopted out of harsh necessity, but the resulting neighborhoods developed a striking charm: intimate townscapes with exceptional vitality and livability, featuring a fine-grained urban fabric comprised of numerous small buildings. Before long, Castejón's post-earthquake spontaneity gave way to more methodical planning measures designed to impose at least some measure of order on the city’s development, but in doing so they invested little effort in changing the character of these post-earthquake neighborhoods.

The Tistaina Canal was extensively rerouted through the devastated center of Ampurias, with the original canal bed filled in to create what is now the [[Avenida de la Memoria]] (Avenue of Memory). The earthquake cottages were gradually replaced by tenement apartments and [[W:Tower block|tower blocks]] completed between 1926 and 1932, many of which still stand and display commemorative plaques noting their origins in the reconstruction effort.

The disaster influenced the development of the cooperative fire insurance system and led to the establishment of the [[Confederal Geological Institute|Instituto de Geología Confederal]], tasked with mapping seismic hazards throughout Balisca. The Yawar Sypa fault was extensively studied in the 1930s-1950s, with trenching excavations revealing evidence of prehistoric earthquakes of comparable magnitude occurring at approximately 300-year intervals.

